Subject: [PATCH 02656/11362] [outer-loop] DiffusionGemma-26B-A4B Layer-B eval
 + 3-way model comparison: fastest raw (parallel diffusion) but
 reasoning-canvas blocks code; gemma-12B stays default

Operator request: compare unsloth/diffusiongemma-26B-A4B-it-GGUF vs gemma-12B + Qwopus-27B on Layer B.

DiffusionGemma is block-diffusion (non-AR) -- standard llama-server cannot generate from it; built
llama-diffusion-cli for the HIP/iGPU backend (NOT the 3090s). It is structurally the FASTEST (denoises
the whole 256-tok canvas in parallel, 18 tok/s effective on a simple prompt, ~4x the AR models) and a
26B-MoE/4B-active. BUT on Layer B it does NOT ground: it is a reasoning model (<|channel>thought) and the
diffusion canvas is FIXED-length, so it fills the canvas with chain-of-thought and never commits to code.
It DID write the exact correct rule (np.sum(grid[63,26:64]==4)==32) on a SIMPLE 'output only code' prompt
-- so the capability is there; the failure is reasoning-vs-fixed-canvas. 4 mitigations failed via
llama-diffusion-cli (bigger canvas loops; no-think directive can't override the trained thought-channel;
channel-priming still denoises reasoning).

Reframe: the project uses DiffusionGemma as an ENERGY-PRIOR/score signal (denoiser logits = -grad E over
tokens; the pending diffusiongemma energy-guided gate), NOT a generator -- verifier-side, Carnot's thesis.
This test ran it off-label as a code generator where its reasoning canvas is a poor fit.

3-way bottom line: gemma-4-12B stays the Layer-B default (grounds the tractable class, easiest to drive,
fastest effective). Qwopus-27B+MTP for harder coding on a fast GPU (MTP native, 80% accept, free 1.5x).
DiffusionGemma for the energy-prior/verifier role or as a fast parallel generator only with template-level
thinking-suppression. The Layer-B wall is digest-shape + glyph-perception, not LLM strength. Adversarial-verify clean.

